{
  "lines": {
    "0": {
      "text": "BOGORAS, THE CHUKCHEE."
    },
    "1": {
      "text": "247"
    },
    "2": {
      "text": "The Chukchee women wear this overcoat over their usual dress when going"
    },
    "3": {
      "text": "for a walk or ride in the open air. It must also be put on in ceremonial"
    },
    "4": {
      "text": "dances and performances."
    },
    "5": {
      "text": "The Koryak women wear"
    },
    "6": {
      "text": "the overcoat with their"
    },
    "7": {
      "text": "ordinary costume, and"
    },
    "8": {
      "text": "consider the combination-"
    },
    "9": {
      "text": "suit as underclothing."
    },
    "10": {
      "text": "The Chukchee women"
    },
    "11": {
      "text": "sometimes have overcoats"
    },
    "12": {
      "text": "made of cotton, but do"
    },
    "13": {
      "text": "not wear these so fre-"
    },
    "14": {
      "text": "quently as the men. In"
    },
    "15": {
      "text": "the Pacific villages the"
    },
    "16": {
      "text": "Chukchee and the Eskimo"
    },
    "17": {
      "text": "young women also have"
    },
    "18": {
      "text": "overcoats made of thin"
    },
    "19": {
      "text": "guts of seal and walrus"
    },
    "20": {
      "text": "carefully bleached and"
    },
    "21": {
      "text": "quite transparent. They"
    },
    "22": {
      "text": "are exceedingly light, and"
    },
    "23": {
      "text": "not ungraceful in appear-"
    },
    "24": {
      "text": "ance (Fig. 180). The"
    },
    "25": {
      "text": "guts are neatly stitched"
    },
    "26": {
      "text": "together in long bands,"
    },
    "27": {
      "text": "and the coat is trimmed"
    },
    "28": {
      "text": "with tassels and small red"
    },
    "29": {
      "text": "tufts of feathers from the"
    },
    "30": {
      "text": "heads of crested auks."
    },
    "31": {
      "text": "It is probable that this"
    },
    "32": {
      "text": "frock or coat belonged"
    },
    "33": {
      "text": "originally to the Eskimo"
    },
    "34": {
      "text": "costume. Even now the"
    },
    "35": {
      "text": "best are brought from"
    },
    "36": {
      "text": "St. Lawrence Island, where"
    },
    "37": {
      "text": "seals are plentiful and"
    },
    "38": {
      "text": "crested auks also abound."
    },
    "39": {
      "text": "The Maritime Chukchee, however, have adopted it as a dress for the great"
    },
    "40": {
      "text": "fall ceremonial. They believe that the sea-god Kereˊtkun and his wife wear",
      "note": "printed with a raised stress mark on the sea-god's name; machine text read it as a slash. Matches the ratified corpus key Kere'tkun -> Kereˊtkun (2-042, 2-045).",
      "class": "print",
      "as_typed": "fall ceremonial. They believe that the sea-god {Kere'tkun} and his wife wear"
    },
    "41": {
      "text": "overcoats of exactly the same shape. Therefore while bringing sacrifices to"
    },
    "42": {
      "text": "Kereˊtkun, the whole family, down to the small children, don this garment in",
      "note": "same name as line 40, resolved via lexicon lookup key Kere'tkun -> Kereˊtkun.",
      "class": "print",
      "as_typed": "{Kere'tkun}, the whole family, down to the small children, don this garment in"
    },
    "43": {
      "text": "imitation of the deity. At all other times these coats are kept in bags."
    }
  },
  "contamination": {
    "blind": true
  }
}
